solution:
If $a \geq b$ then $\alpha \geq \beta$ and analogously if $a \leq b$ then we have $\alpha \leq \beta$.
So we have $(a-b)(\alpha-\beta) \geq 0$, i.e. we have
$$
a \alpha+b \beta \geq a \beta+b \alpha
$$
i.e.
$$
\begin{equation*}
\frac{a}{\beta}+\frac{b}{\alpha} \geq \frac{a}{\alpha}+\frac{b}{\beta} \tag{1}
\end{equation*}
$$

Analogously we have
$$
\begin{equation*}
\frac{a}{\gamma}+\frac{c}{\alpha} \geq \frac{a}{\alpha}+\frac{c}{\gamma} \tag{2}
\end{equation*}
$$
and
$$
\begin{equation*}
\frac{c}{\beta}+\frac{b}{\gamma} \geq \frac{c}{\beta}+\frac{b}{\gamma} \tag{3}
\end{equation*}
$$

Adding (1), (2) and (3) we obtain the required inequality.

Equality holds when $a = b = c$ and $\alpha = \beta = \gamma$, i.e., when the triangle is equilateral. In this case, both sides of the inequality are equal, and the value $C = 2$ is the largest possible for which the inequality always holds (i.e., it gives the minimum value of $C$).

Therefore, the answer is $C = 2$.

