\end{equation*}
$$

Using (1) and $A M \geq H M$ we obtain
$$
\frac{b+c}{\alpha}+\frac{c+a}{\beta}+\frac{a+b}{\gamma} \geq \frac{4 s}{3}\left(\frac{1}{\alpha}+\frac{1}{\beta}+\frac{1}{\gamma}\right) \geq \frac{4 s}{3} \cdot \frac{9}{\alpha+\beta+\gamma}=\frac{12 s}{\pi}
$$

Equality holds when $a = b = c$, i.e., the triangle is equilateral. In this case, $s = \frac{3a}{2}$ and $\alpha = \beta = \gamma = \frac{\pi}{3}$, so the inequality becomes an equality. This gives the minimum value of the left-hand side, so $C = \frac{12}{\pi}$ is the maximal constant for which the inequality always holds.

Therefore, the answer is $C = \frac{12}{\pi}$.

