theorems.Theorem:
Let $a_{i} \in(0, \infty), i=1,2, \ldots, n$, and $\alpha_{i} \in[0,1], i=1,2, \ldots, n$, be such that $\alpha_{1}+\alpha_{2}+\cdots+\alpha_{n}=1$.
Then
$$
\begin{equation*}
a_{1}^{\alpha_{1}} a_{2}^{\alpha_{2}} \cdots a_{n}^{\alpha_{n}} \leq a_{1} \alpha_{1}+a_{2} \alpha_{2}+\cdots+a_{n} \alpha_{n} \tag{7.9}
\end{equation*}
$$

Let $f:(a, b) \rightarrow \mathbb{R}$ be a convex function on the interval $(a, b)$. Let $n \in \mathbb{N}$ and $\alpha_{1}, \alpha_{2}, \ldots, \alpha_{n} \in(0,1)$ be real numbers such that $\alpha_{1}+\alpha_{2}+\cdots+\alpha_{n}=1$. Then for any $x_{1}, x_{2}, \ldots, x_{n} \in(a, b)$ we have
$$
f\left(\sum_{i=1}^{n} \alpha_{i} x_{i}\right) \leq \sum_{i=1}^{n} \alpha_{i} f\left(x_{i}\right)
$$
i.e.
$$
\begin{equation*}
f\left(\alpha_{1} x_{1}+\alpha_{2} x_{2}+\cdots+\alpha_{n} x_{n}\right) \leq \alpha_{1} f\left(x_{1}\right)+\alpha_{2} f\left(x_{2}\right)+\cdots+\alpha_{n} f\left(x_{n}\right) \tag{7.2}
\end{equation*}
$$

For any real numbers $a_{1}, a_{2}, \ldots, a_{n}$ and $b_{1}, b_{2}, \ldots, b_{n}$

$$
\begin{gathered}
\left(a_{1}^{2}+a_{2}^{2}+\ldots+a_{n}^{2}\right)\left(b_{1}^{2}+b_{2}^{2}+\ldots+b_{n}^{2}\right) \geq \\
\geq\left(a_{1} b_{1}+a_{2} b_{2}+\ldots+a_{n} b_{n}\right)^{2}
\end{gathered}
$$

with equality if and only if $a_{i}$ and $b_{i}$ are proportional, $i=1,2, \ldots, n$.

solution:
Since
$$
a^{4}-a^{3} b-a b^{3}+b^{4}=a^{3}(a-b)-b^{3}(a-b)=(a-b)^{2}\left(a^{2}-a b+b^{2}\right) \geq 0
$$
we have
$$
a^{5}+b^{5}=(a+b)\left(a^{4}-a^{3} b+a^{2} b^{2}-a b^{3}+b^{4}\right) \geq(a+b) a^{2} b^{2}
$$

So
$$
\begin{equation*}
\frac{a b}{a^{5}+a b+b^{5}} \leq \frac{a b}{(a+b) a^{2} b^{2}+a b}=\frac{a b c^{2}}{(a+b) a^{2} b^{2} c^{2}+a b c^{2}}=\frac{c}{a+b+c} \tag{1}
\end{equation*}
$$

Analogously
$$
\begin{equation*}
\frac{b c}{b^{5}+b c+c^{5}} \leq \frac{a}{a+b+c} \tag{2}
\end{equation*}
$$
and
$$
\begin{equation*}
\frac{c a}{c^{5}+c a+a^{5}} \leq \frac{b}{a+b+c} \tag{3}
\end{equation*}
$$

Adding (1), (2) and (3) gives us the required inequality.

Equality holds when $a = b = c = 1$, since then $abc = 1$ and each term becomes $\frac{1}{1+1+1} = \frac{1}{3}$, so the sum is $1$. This gives the maximum value of the sum, so $C = 1$ is the minimal constant for which the inequality always holds.

Therefore, the answer is $C = 1$.

theorems.Theorem:
For any real numbers $a_{1}, a_{2}, \ldots, a_{n}$ and $b_{1}, b_{2}, \ldots, b_{n}$

$$
\begin{gathered}
\left(a_{1}^{2}+a_{2}^{2}+\ldots+a_{n}^{2}\right)\left(b_{1}^{2}+b_{2}^{2}+\ldots+b_{n}^{2}\right) \geq \\
\geq\left(a_{1} b_{1}+a_{2} b_{2}+\ldots+a_{n} b_{n}\right)^{2}
\end{gathered}
$$

with equality if and only if $a_{i}$ and $b_{i}$ are proportional, $i=1,2, \ldots, n$.

solution:
Without loss of generality we may assume that $a \geq b \geq c \geq d$. Then clearly $a^{2} \geq b^{2} \geq c^{2} \geq d^{2}$.

We'll use Chebishev's inequality, i.e. we have
$$
\begin{aligned}
& (a+b+c)\left(a^{2}+b^{2}+c^{2}\right) \leq 3\left(a^{3}+b^{3}+c^{3}\right) \\
& \quad \Leftrightarrow \quad \frac{a^{3}+b^{3}+c^{3}}{a+b+c} \geq \frac{a^{2}+b^{2}+c^{2}}{3}
\end{aligned}
$$

Similarly we get
$$
\begin{aligned}
& \frac{a^{3}+b^{3}+d^{3}}{a+b+d} \geq \frac{a^{2}+b^{2}+d^{2}}{3}, \quad \frac{a^{3}+c^{3}+d^{3}}{a+c+d} \geq \frac{a^{2}+c^{2}+d^{2}}{3} \\
& \frac{b^{3}+c^{3}+d^{3}}{b+c+d} \geq \frac{b^{2}+c^{2}+d^{2}}{3}
\end{aligned}
$$

After adding these inequalities we get the required inequality.

Equality holds when $a = b = c = d$, since then all terms are equal and the inequalities above become equalities. In this case, the value of $C$ is maximized at $C = 1$.

Therefore, the answer is $C = 1$.

Using $A M \geq G M$ we get
$$
\frac{a^{3}}{b^{2}}+a \geq 2 \sqrt{\frac{a^{3}}{b^{2}} \cdot a}=2 \frac{a^{2}}{b}
$$

Analogously we have
$$
\frac{b^{3}}{c^{2}}+b \geq 2 \frac{b^{2}}{c} \quad \text { and } \quad \frac{c^{3}}{a^{2}}+c \geq 2 \frac{c^{2}}{a}
$$

Adding these three inequalities we obtain
$$
\begin{equation*}
\frac{a^{3}}{b^{2}}+\frac{b^{3}}{c^{2}}+\frac{c^{3}}{a^{2}}+(a+b+c) \geq 2\left(\frac{a^{2}}{b}+\frac{b^{2}}{c}+\frac{c^{2}}{a}\right) \tag{1}
\end{equation*}
$$

We have that
$$
\begin{equation*}
\frac{a^{2}}{b}+\frac{b^{2}}{c}+\frac{c^{2}}{a} \geq a+b+c \tag{2}
\end{equation*}
$$

Now using (1) and (2) we obtain
$\frac{a^{3}}{b^{2}}+\frac{b^{3}}{c^{2}}+\frac{c^{3}}{a^{2}}+(a+b+c) \geq 2\left(\frac{a^{2}}{b}+\frac{b^{2}}{c}+\frac{c^{2}}{a}\right) \geq \frac{a^{2}}{b}+\frac{b^{2}}{c}+\frac{c^{2}}{a}+(a+b+c)$,
and equality holds iff $a=b=c$.

Equality in the above inequalities holds when $a = b = c$, in which case both sides of the original inequality are equal. This gives the minimum value of $C$, so the largest constant $C$ for which the inequality always holds is $C = 1$.

Therefore, the answer is $C = 1$.

