$$

Using (3) and (4) we obtain
$$
\begin{equation*}
x+y+z-x y z \geq \sqrt{3}-\frac{1}{3 \sqrt{3}}=\frac{8}{3 \sqrt{3}} \tag{5}
\end{equation*}
$$

Finally using (1), (2) and (5) we get
$$
\frac{x}{1+x^{2}}+\frac{y}{1+y^{2}}+\frac{z}{1+z^{2}}=\frac{2}{(x+y)(y+z)(z+x)}=\frac{2}{x+y+z-x y z} \leq \frac{3 \sqrt{3}}{4}
$$

Equality occurs when $x = y = z = \frac{1}{\sqrt{3}}$, since then $xy + yz + zx = 3 \cdot \left(\frac{1}{\sqrt{3}}\right)^2 = 1$ and $x + y + z - xyz = \sqrt{3} - \frac{1}{3\sqrt{3}} = \frac{8}{3\sqrt{3}}$, so the maximum value of the sum is $C = \frac{3 \sqrt{3}}{4}$.

Therefore, the answer is $C = \frac{3 \sqrt{3}}{4}$.

