theorems.Theorem:
For any real numbers $a_{1}, a_{2}, \ldots, a_{n}$ and $b_{1}, b_{2}, \ldots, b_{n}$

$$
\begin{gathered}
\left(a_{1}^{2}+a_{2}^{2}+\ldots+a_{n}^{2}\right)\left(b_{1}^{2}+b_{2}^{2}+\ldots+b_{n}^{2}\right) \geq \\
\geq\left(a_{1} b_{1}+a_{2} b_{2}+\ldots+a_{n} b_{n}\right)^{2}
\end{gathered}
$$

with equality if and only if $a_{i}$ and $b_{i}$ are proportional, $i=1,2, \ldots, n$.

Let $f:[a, b] \rightarrow \mathbb{R}$ and $g:[a, b] \rightarrow \mathbb{R}$ be functions such that:
(i) $f$ and $g$ are continuous on $[a, b]$ and $f(a)=g(a)$;
(ii) $f$ and $g$ are differentiable on $(a, b)$;
(iii) $f^{\prime}(x)>g^{\prime}(x)$, for all $x \in(a, b)$.

Then, for all $x \in(a, b)$, we have $f(x)>g(x)$.

For any real numbers $a_{1}, a_{2}, \ldots, a_{n}$ and $b_{1}, b_{2}, \ldots, b_{n}$

$$
\begin{gathered}
\left(a_{1}^{2}+a_{2}^{2}+\ldots+a_{n}^{2}\right)\left(b_{1}^{2}+b_{2}^{2}+\ldots+b_{n}^{2}\right) \geq \\
\geq\left(a_{1} b_{1}+a_{2} b_{2}+\ldots+a_{n} b_{n}\right)^{2}
\end{gathered}
$$

with equality if and only if $a_{i}$ and $b_{i}$ are proportional, $i=1,2, \ldots, n$.

solution:
Without loss of generality we may assume $a \geq b \geq c$.
Let's denote $u=\frac{a+b}{2}$ and $v=\frac{a-b}{2}$.
We easily obtain $a=u+v$ and $b=u-v$.
We have $a b=u^{2}-v^{2} \geq c^{2}$ which implies $2 u^{2}-2 c^{2}-v^{2} \geq 0$.
Now we have
$$
\begin{align*}
a^{2} b^{2}+b^{2} c^{2}+c^{2} a^{2} & =c^{2}\left(a^{2}+b^{2}\right)+a^{2} b^{2}=c^{2}\left(2 u^{2}+2 v^{2}\right)+\left(u^{2}-v^{2}\right)^{2} \\
& =-v^{2}\left(2 u^{2}-2 c^{2}-v^{2}\right)+u^{4}+2 c^{2} u^{2} \leq u^{4}+2 c^{2} u^{2} \tag{1}
\end{align*}
$$

Also
$$
\begin{equation*}
a^{2}+b^{2}+c^{2}=2 u^{2}+2 v^{2}+c^{2} \geq 2 u^{2}+c^{2} \tag{2}
\end{equation*}
$$

We'll show that
$$
\begin{equation*}
2\left(u^{4}+2 c^{2} u^{2}\right)+3 \leq 3\left(2 u^{2}+c^{2}\right) \tag{3}
\end{equation*}
$$

From $a+b+c=3$ we have $c=3-2 u$.
Now inequality (3) is equivalent to
$$
\begin{aligned}
& 2 u^{4}+4(3-2 u)^{2} u^{2}+3 \leq 6 u^{2}+3(3-2 u)^{2} \\
& \quad \Leftrightarrow \quad 3 u^{4}-8 u^{3}+3 u^{2}+6 u-4 \leq 0 \quad \Leftrightarrow \quad(u-1)^{2}\left(3 u^{2}-2 u-4\right) \leq 0
\end{aligned}
$$

Since $2 u \leq 3$ we easily deduce that $3 u^{2}-2 u-4 \leq 0$. So inequality (3) holds.

Combining (1), (2) and (3) we obtain the required result.

Equality holds if and only if $a = b = c = 1$, since in this case $a + b + c = 3$ and the expressions on both sides of the inequality are equal. This gives the maximum value of $C$ for which the inequality always holds, which is $C = 3$.

Therefore, the answer is $C = 3$.

Applying the Cauchy-Schwarz inequality for
$$
\begin{array}{lll}
a_{1}=\sqrt{b+c}, & a_{2}=\sqrt{c+a}, & a_{3}=\sqrt{a+b} \\
b_{1}=\frac{1}{\sqrt{b+c}}, & b_{2}=\frac{1}{\sqrt{c+a}}, & b_{3}=\frac{1}{\sqrt{a+b}}
\end{array}
$$
gives us
$$
((b+c)+(c+a)+(a+b))\left(\frac{1}{b+c}+\frac{1}{c+a}+\frac{1}{a+b}\right) \geq(1+1+1)^{2}=9
$$
i.e.
$$
\begin{aligned}
& 2(a+b+c)\left(\frac{1}{b+c}+\frac{1}{c+a}+\frac{1}{a+b}\right) \geq 9 \\
& \quad \Leftrightarrow \quad \frac{a+b+c}{b+c}+\frac{a+b+c}{c+a}+\frac{a+b+c}{a+b} \geq \frac{9}{2} \\
& \quad \Leftrightarrow \quad \frac{a}{b+c}+\frac{b}{c+a}+\frac{c}{a+b} \geq \frac{9}{2}-3=\frac{3}{2}
\end{aligned}
$$

Equality occurs iff $(b+c)^{2}=(c+a)^{2}=(a+b)^{2}$, i.e. iff $a=b=c$.2 We'll use Chebishev's inequality.
Assume that $a \geq b \geq c$; then $\frac{1}{b+c} \geq \frac{1}{c+a} \geq \frac{1}{a+b}$.
Now by Chebishev's inequality we get
$$
\begin{equation*}
(a+b+c)\left(\frac{1}{b+c}+\frac{1}{c+a}+\frac{1}{a+b}\right) \leq 3\left(\frac{a}{b+c}+\frac{b}{c+a}+\frac{c}{a+b}\right) \tag{1}
\end{equation*}
$$

Note that
$$
\begin{aligned}
(a+b+c)\left(\frac{1}{b+c}+\frac{1}{c+a}+\frac{1}{a+b}\right)= & \frac{1}{2}((b+c)+(c+a)+(a+b)) \\
& \times\left(\frac{1}{b+c}+\frac{1}{c+a}+\frac{1}{a+b}\right)
\end{aligned}
$$

Since $A M \geq H M$ (the same thing in this case with Cauchy-Schwarz) we have
$$
((b+c)+(c+a)+(a+b))\left(\frac{1}{b+c}+\frac{1}{c+a}+\frac{1}{a+b}\right) \geq 9
$$

Therefore
$$
\begin{equation*}
(a+b+c)\left(\frac{1}{b+c}+\frac{1}{c+a}+\frac{1}{a+b}\right) \geq \frac{9}{2} \tag{2}
\end{equation*}
$$

By (1) and (2) we obtain
$$
3\left(\frac{a}{b+c}+\frac{b}{c+a}+\frac{c}{a+b}\right) \geq \frac{9}{2}, \quad \text { i.e. } \quad \frac{a}{b+c}+\frac{b}{c+a}+\frac{c}{a+b} \geq \frac{3}{2}
$$

Equality occurs when $a = b = c$, in which case $\frac{a}{b+c}+\frac{b}{c+a}+\frac{c}{a+b} = \frac{3}{2}$. Thus, the minimum value of the expression is $\frac{3}{2}$, so $C = \frac{3}{2}$ is the largest constant for which the inequality always holds.

Therefore, the answer is $C = \frac{3}{2}$.

