theorems.Theorem:
For any real numbers $a_{1}, a_{2}, \ldots, a_{n}$ and $b_{1}, b_{2}, \ldots, b_{n}$

$$
\begin{gathered}
\left(a_{1}^{2}+a_{2}^{2}+\ldots+a_{n}^{2}\right)\left(b_{1}^{2}+b_{2}^{2}+\ldots+b_{n}^{2}\right) \geq \\
\geq\left(a_{1} b_{1}+a_{2} b_{2}+\ldots+a_{n} b_{n}\right)^{2}
\end{gathered}
$$

with equality if and only if $a_{i}$ and $b_{i}$ are proportional, $i=1,2, \ldots, n$.

solution:
At the point of incidence $a=b=c=1$ we have $5 a^{2}+4(b+c)+3=16$. By the inequality $A M \geq G M$ we get
$$
\begin{aligned}
\sqrt[4]{5 a^{2}+4(b+c)+3} & =\frac{\sqrt[4]{\left(5 a^{2}+4(b+c)+3\right) \cdot 16^{3}}}{8} \\
& \leq \frac{1}{32}\left(5 a^{2}+4(b+c)+3+3 \cdot 16\right) \\
& =\frac{5 a^{2}+4(b+c)+51}{32}
\end{aligned}
$$

Similarly we obtain
$$
\sqrt[4]{5 b^{2}+4(c+a)+3} \leq \frac{5 b^{2}+4(c+a)+51}{32}
$$
and
$$
\sqrt[4]{5 c^{2}+4(a+b)+3} \leq \frac{5 c^{2}+4(a+b)+51}{32}
$$

Adding the last three inequalities gives us
$$
\begin{aligned}
& \sqrt[4]{5 a^{2}+4(b+c)+3}+\sqrt[4]{5 b^{2}+4(c+a)+3}+\sqrt[4]{5 c^{2}+4(a+b)+3} \\
& \quad \leq \frac{5\left(a^{2}+b^{2}+c^{2}\right)+8(a+b+c)+153}{32}
\end{aligned}
$$

Since $a^{2}+b^{2}+c^{2}=3$ we have $a+b+c \leq \sqrt{3\left(a^{2}+b^{2}+c^{2}\right)}=3$, and by the last inequality we obtain
$$
\begin{aligned}
& \sqrt[4]{5 a^{2}+4(b+c)+3}+\sqrt[4]{5 b^{2}+4(c+a)+3}+\sqrt[4]{5 c^{2}+4(a+b)+3} \\
& \quad \leq \frac{5 \cdot 3+8 \cdot 3+153}{32}=\frac{192}{32}=6
\end{aligned}
$$

Equality occurs if and only if $a = b = c = 1$, which is when $a^2 + b^2 + c^2 = 3$ and $a + b + c = 3$. Thus, the maximum value of the sum is $C = 6$.

Therefore, the answer is $C = 6$.

We'll use the Cauchy-Schwarz inequality, i.e.
$$
\begin{equation*}
\left(a_{1}^{2}+a_{2}^{2}+a_{3}^{2}\right)\left(b_{1}^{2}+b_{2}^{2}+b_{3}^{2}\right) \geq\left(a_{1} b_{1}+a_{2} b_{2}+a_{3} b_{3}\right)^{2} \tag{1}
\end{equation*}
$$

Let
$$
\begin{aligned}
& a_{1}=\sqrt{a^{2}\left(b^{2}+1\right)}, \quad a_{2}=\sqrt{b^{2}\left(c^{2}+1\right)}, \quad a_{3}=\sqrt{c^{2}\left(a^{2}+1\right)} \quad \text { and } \\
& b_{1}=\sqrt{\frac{a}{b^{2}+1}}, \quad b_{2}=\sqrt{\frac{b}{c^{2}+1}}, \quad b_{3}=\sqrt{\frac{c}{a^{2}+1}} .
\end{aligned}
$$

Then using (1) we get
$$
\begin{aligned}
& \left(a^{2}\left(b^{2}+1\right)+b^{2}\left(c^{2}+1\right)+c^{2}\left(a^{2}+1\right)\right)\left(\frac{a}{b^{2}+1}+\frac{b}{c^{2}+1}+\frac{c}{a^{2}+1}\right) \\
& \quad \geq(a \sqrt{a}+b \sqrt{b}+c \sqrt{c})^{2}
\end{aligned}
$$
i.e.
$$
\frac{a}{b^{2}+1}+\frac{b}{c^{2}+1}+\frac{c}{a^{2}+1} \geq \frac{(a \sqrt{a}+b \sqrt{b}+c \sqrt{c})^{2}}{a^{2}\left(b^{2}+1\right)+b^{2}\left(c^{2}+1\right)+c^{2}\left(a^{2}+1\right)}
$$

So it suffices to show that
$$
a^{2}\left(b^{2}+1\right)+b^{2}\left(c^{2}+1\right)+c^{2}\left(a^{2}+1\right) \leq \frac{4}{3}
$$

From the obvious inequality $\left(a^{2}-b^{2}\right)^{2}+\left(b^{2}-c^{2}\right)^{2}+\left(c^{2}-a^{2}\right)^{2} \geq 0$ we deduce that
$$
\begin{equation*}
a^{2} b^{2}+b^{2} c^{2}+c^{2} a^{2} \leq a^{4}+b^{4}+c^{4} \tag{2}
\end{equation*}
$$

Now we have
$$
\begin{aligned}
a^{2} & \left(b^{2}+1\right)+b^{2}\left(c^{2}+1\right)+c^{2}\left(a^{2}+1\right) \\
& =a^{2}+b^{2}+c^{2}+a^{2} b^{2}+b^{2} c^{2}+c^{2} a^{2} \\
& =a^{2}+b^{2}+c^{2}+\frac{3\left(a^{2} b^{2}+b^{2} c^{2}+c^{2} a^{2}\right)}{3} \\
& \stackrel{(2)}{\leq} a^{2}+b^{2}+c^{2}+\frac{2\left(a^{2} b^{2}+b^{2} c^{2}+c^{2} a^{2}\right)+a^{4}+b^{4}+c^{4}}{3} \\
& =a^{2}+b^{2}+c^{2}+\frac{\left(a^{2}+b^{2}+c^{2}\right)^{2}}{3}=1+\frac{1}{3}=\frac{4}{3}
\end{aligned}
$$
as required.

Equality occurs when $a = b = c = \frac{1}{\sqrt{3}}$, which also maximizes $C$. Thus, the maximum value of $C$ for which the inequality always holds is $C = \frac{3}{4}$.

Therefore, the answer is $C = \frac{3}{4}$.

