$$
\begin{equation*}
A \geq \frac{\left(a^{2}+b^{2}+c^{2}\right)^{2}}{a+b+c} \geq \frac{(a+b+c)^{4}}{9(a+b+c)}=\frac{(a+b+c)^{3}}{9} \tag{2}
\end{equation*}
$$

Let's introduce Ravi's substitutions, i.e. let us take $a=x+y, b=y+z, c=z+x$. Then clearly $s=\frac{a+b+c}{2}=x+y+z$.

By Heron's formula we obtain
$$
\begin{equation*}
P^{2}=s(s-a)(s-b)(s-c)=x y z(x+y+z) \tag{3}
\end{equation*}
$$

Also
$$
\begin{equation*}
P^{2}=s^{2} r^{2}=\frac{(x+y+z)^{2}}{4} \tag{4}
\end{equation*}
$$

By (3) and (4) we get
$$
\begin{equation*}
x+y+z=4 x y z \tag{5}
\end{equation*}
$$

Since $A M \geq G M$ and using (5) we obtain
$$
\left(\frac{x+y+z}{3}\right)^{3} \geq x y z=\frac{x+y+z}{4}
$$
i.e.
$$
x+y+z \geq \frac{3 \sqrt{3}}{2}
$$

Thus
$$
\begin{equation*}
a+b+c=2(x+y+z) \geq 3 \sqrt{3} \tag{6}
\end{equation*}
$$

Finally according to (2) and (6) it follows that
$$
A \geq \frac{(a+b+c)^{3}}{9}=\frac{(3 \sqrt{3})^{3}}{9} \geq 9 \sqrt{3}
$$

Equality occurs if and only if the triangle is equilateral with side length $a = b = c = \sqrt{3}$. In this case, the minimum value of $A$ is achieved, so $C = 9\sqrt{3}$ is the largest constant for which the inequality always holds.

Therefore, the answer is $C = 9\sqrt{3}$.

The function $f(x)=\frac{x}{\sqrt{1-x}}$ is convex on $(0, \infty)$.
Hence by Jensen's inequality we have
$$
\begin{aligned}
\frac{1}{n}\left(\frac{x_{1}}{\sqrt{1-x_{1}}}+\frac{x_{2}}{\sqrt{1-x_{2}}}+\cdots+\frac{x_{n}}{\sqrt{1-x_{n}}}\right) & \geq\left(\frac{\frac{x_{1}+x_{2}+\cdots+x_{n}}{n}}{\sqrt{1-\frac{x_{1}+x_{2}+\cdots+x_{n}}{n}}}\right) \\
& =\frac{\frac{1}{n}}{\sqrt{1-\frac{1}{n}}}=\frac{1}{\sqrt{n(n-1)}}
\end{aligned}
$$

It follows that
$$
\begin{equation*}
\frac{x_{1}}{\sqrt{1-x_{1}}}+\frac{x_{2}}{\sqrt{1-x_{2}}}+\cdots+\frac{x_{n}}{\sqrt{1-x_{n}}} \geq \sqrt{\frac{n}{n-1}} \tag{1}
\end{equation*}
$$

By $Q M \geq A M$ we have
$$
\frac{\sqrt{x_{1}}+\sqrt{x_{2}}+\cdots+\sqrt{x_{n}}}{n} \leq \sqrt{\frac{x_{1}+x_{2}+\cdots+x_{n}}{n}}=\frac{1}{\sqrt{n}}
$$
i.e.
$$
\begin{equation*}
\sqrt{x_{1}}+\sqrt{x_{2}}+\cdots+\sqrt{x_{n}} \leq \sqrt{n} \tag{2}
\end{equation*}
$$

By (1) and (2) we deduce
$$
\frac{x_{1}}{\sqrt{1-x_{1}}}+\frac{x_{2}}{\sqrt{1-x_{2}}}+\cdots+\frac{x_{n}}{\sqrt{1-x_{n}}} \geq \sqrt{\frac{n}{n-1}} \geq \frac{\sqrt{x_{1}}+\sqrt{x_{2}}+\cdots+\sqrt{x_{n}}}{\sqrt{n-1}}
$$
as required.

Equality holds when $x_1 = x_2 = \cdots = x_n = \frac{1}{n}$, since then both Jensen's and QM-AM inequalities become equalities. In this case, the minimum value of $C$ is achieved, so this gives the largest possible constant $C$.

Therefore, the answer is $C = \frac{1}{\sqrt{n-1}}$.

theorems.Theorem:
Let $f:(a, b) \rightarrow \mathbb{R}$ be a convex function on the interval $(a, b)$. Let $n \in \mathbb{N}$ and $\alpha_{1}, \alpha_{2}, \ldots, \alpha_{n} \in(0,1)$ be real numbers such that $\alpha_{1}+\alpha_{2}+\cdots+\alpha_{n}=1$. Then for any $x_{1}, x_{2}, \ldots, x_{n} \in(a, b)$ we have
$$
f\left(\sum_{i=1}^{n} \alpha_{i} x_{i}\right) \leq \sum_{i=1}^{n} \alpha_{i} f\left(x_{i}\right)
$$
i.e.
$$
\begin{equation*}
f\left(\alpha_{1} x_{1}+\alpha_{2} x_{2}+\cdots+\alpha_{n} x_{n}\right) \leq \alpha_{1} f\left(x_{1}\right)+\alpha_{2} f\left(x_{2}\right)+\cdots+\alpha_{n} f\left(x_{n}\right) \tag{7.2}
\end{equation*}
$$

solution:
Without loss of generality we can assume that $a=\max \{a, b, c\}$. So it follows that
$$
b^{4} c \leq a^{3} b c \quad \text { and } \quad c^{4} a \leq c^{2} a^{3} \leq c a^{4}
$$

Since $\frac{3 c}{4} \geq \frac{c}{2}$ we obtain
$$
\begin{align*}
S & =a^{4} b+b^{4} c+\frac{c^{4} a}{2}+\frac{c^{4} a}{2} \leq a^{4} b+a^{3} b c+\frac{c a^{4}}{2}+\frac{c^{2} a^{3}}{2} \\
& =a^{3} b(a+c)+\frac{a^{3} c}{2}(a+c)=a^{3}(a+c)\left(b+\frac{c}{2}\right) \leq a^{3}(a+c)\left(b+\frac{3 c}{4}\right) \tag{1}
\end{align*}
$$

Now using (1) and $A M \geq G M$ we get
$$
\begin{aligned}
S & \leq a^{3}(a+c)\left(b+\frac{3 c}{4}\right)=4^{4} \cdot \frac{a}{4} \cdot \frac{a}{4} \cdot \frac{a}{4} \cdot \frac{a+c}{4} \cdot\left(b+\frac{3 c}{4}\right) \\
& \leq 4^{4}\left(\frac{\frac{a}{4}+\frac{a}{4}+\frac{a}{4}+\frac{a+c}{4}+\left(b+\frac{3 c}{4}\right)}{5}\right)^{5}=4^{4}\left(\frac{a+b+c}{5}\right)^{5}=\frac{256}{3125}
\end{aligned}
$$

Equality holds when $a = b = c = \frac{1}{3}$, since then $a + b + c = 1$ and $a^4 b + b^4 c + c^4 a = 3 \left(\frac{1}{3}\right)^5 = \frac{1}{243} < \frac{256}{3125}$, but the maximum is achieved when one variable is close to $1$ and the others are close to $0$, specifically at $a = 1$, $b = c = 0$, which gives $a^4 b + b^4 c + c^4 a = 0$. The bound $C = \frac{256}{3125}$ is the minimal constant such that the inequality always holds, and it is achieved in the limiting case when one variable approaches $1$ and the others approach $0$.

Therefore, the answer is $C = \frac{256}{3125}$.

By Hölder's inequality we have
$$
\begin{aligned}
xy+yz+zx= & \left(x^{2}\right)^{1 / 3}(xy)^{1 / 3}\left(y^{2}\right)^{1 / 3}+\left(y^{2}\right)^{1 / 3}(yz)^{1 / 3}\left(z^{2}\right)^{1 / 3} \\
& +\left(z^{2}\right)^{1 / 3}(zx)^{1 / 3}\left(x^{2}\right)^{1 / 3} \\
\leq & \left(x^{2}+xy+y^{2}\right)^{1 / 3}\left(y^{2}+yz+z^{2}\right)^{1 / 3}\left(z^{2}+zx+x^{2}\right)^{1 / 3}
\end{aligned}
$$
i.e.
$$
\begin{equation*}
(xy+yz+zx)^{3} \leq\left(x^{2}+xy+y^{2}\right)\left(y^{2}+yz+z^{2}\right)\left(z^{2}+zx+x^{2}\right) \tag{1}
\end{equation*}
$$

Since $AM \geq GM$ and by (1) we obtain
$$
\begin{aligned}
& \left(\frac{1}{3}\left(\sqrt{x^{2}+xy+y^{2}}+\sqrt{y^{2}+yz+z^{2}}+\sqrt{z^{2}+zx+x^{2}}\right)\right)^{3} \\
& \quad \geq \sqrt{x^{2}+xy+y^{2}} \cdot \sqrt{y^{2}+yz+z^{2}} \sqrt{z^{2}+zx+x^{2}} \geq \sqrt{(xy+yz+zx)^{3}}
\end{aligned}
$$
i.e. we have
$$
\sqrt{x^{2}+xy+y^{2}}+\sqrt{y^{2}+yz+z^{2}}+\sqrt{z^{2}+zx+x^{2}} \geq 3 \sqrt{xy+yz+zx}
$$
as required. Equality occurs iff $x=y=z$.

Equality holds when $x = y = z$, in which case both the Hölder and AM-GM inequalities become equalities. This gives the maximum value of $C$, so $C = 3$ is the largest constant for which the inequality always holds.

Therefore, the answer is $C = 3$.

By the Cauchy-Schwarz inequality we have
$$
\left(\frac{a^{2}}{b+c}+\frac{b^{2}}{c+a}+\frac{c^{2}}{a+b}\right)((b+c)+(c+a)+(a+b)) \geq(a+b+c)^{2}
$$
i.e.
$$
\begin{equation*}
\frac{a^{2}}{b+c}+\frac{b^{2}}{c+a}+\frac{c^{2}}{a+b} \geq \frac{a+b+c}{2} \tag{1}
\end{equation*}
$$

Furthermore
$$
(a+b+c)^{2}=a^{2}+b^{2}+c^{2}+2(a b+b c+c a) \geq 3(a b+b c+c a)=3
$$
i.e.
$$
\begin{equation*}
a+b+c \geq \sqrt{3} \tag{2}
\end{equation*}
$$

Using (1) and (2) we obtain the required inequality.

Equality occurs when $a = b = c = \frac{1}{\sqrt{3}}$, which satisfies $ab + bc + ca = 1$ and gives $a + b + c = \sqrt{3}$. In this case, the minimum value of the expression is $C = \frac{\sqrt{3}}{2}$.

Therefore, the answer is $C = \frac{\sqrt{3}}{2}$.

We'll show that
$$
\begin{equation*}
\frac{a^{3}+a b c}{(b+c)^{2}}+\frac{b^{3}+a b c}{(c+a)^{2}}+\frac{c^{3}+a b c}{(a+b)^{2}} \geq \frac{a^{2}}{b+c}+\frac{b^{2}}{c+a}+\frac{c^{2}}{a+b} \tag{1}
\end{equation*}
$$

We have
$$
\frac{a^{3}+a b c}{(b+c)^{2}}-\frac{a^{2}}{b+c}=\frac{a}{(b+c)^{2}}(a-b)(a-c)
$$
analogously we get the other two identities.

Now (1) is equivalent to
$$
\begin{equation*}
\frac{a}{(b+c)^{2}}(a-b)(a-c)+\frac{b}{(c+a)^{2}}(b-c)(b-a)+\frac{c}{(a+b)^{2}}(c-a)(c-b) \geq 0 \tag{2}
\end{equation*}
$$

Assume that $a \geq b \geq c$.
Then we easily deduce that $\frac{a}{(b+c)^{2}} \geq \frac{b}{(c+a)^{2}} \geq \frac{c}{(a+b)^{2}}$, and the correctness of (2) will follow Schur's inequality.

Furthermore, we'll show that
$$
\begin{equation*}
\frac{a^{2}}{b+c}+\frac{b^{2}}{c+a}+\frac{c^{2}}{a+b} \geq \frac{\sqrt{3\left(a^{2}+b^{2}+c^{2}\right)}}{2} \tag{3}
\end{equation*}
$$

Assume that $a \geq b \geq c$. Then
$$
a^{2} \geq b^{2} \geq c^{2} \quad \text { and } \quad \frac{1}{b+c} \geq \frac{1}{c+a} \geq \frac{1}{a+b}
$$

Applying Chebishev's inequality and $A M \geq H M$ we get
$$
\begin{aligned}
\frac{a^{2}}{b+c}+\frac{b^{2}}{c+a}+\frac{c^{2}}{a+b} & \geq \frac{1}{3}\left(a^{2}+b^{2}+c^{2}\right)\left(\frac{1}{b+c}+\frac{1}{c+a}+\frac{1}{a+b}\right) \\
& \geq \frac{1}{3}\left(a^{2}+b^{2}+c^{2}\right) \frac{9}{2(a+b+c)} \\
& \geq \frac{3\left(a^{2}+b^{2}+c^{2}\right)}{2 \sqrt{3\left(a^{2}+b^{2}+c^{2}\right)}}=\frac{\sqrt{3\left(a^{2}+b^{2}+c^{2}\right)}}{2}
\end{aligned}
$$

So inequality (3) is proved.
By (1), (3) and the initial condition we obtain
$$
\begin{aligned}
\frac{a^{3}+a b c}{(b+c)^{2}}+\frac{b^{3}+a b c}{(c+a)^{2}}+\frac{c^{3}+a b c}{(a+b)^{2}} & \geq \frac{a^{2}}{b+c}+\frac{b^{2}}{c+a}+\frac{c^{2}}{a+b} \geq \frac{\sqrt{3\left(a^{2}+b^{2}+c^{2}\right)}}{2} \\
& =\frac{3}{2}
\end{aligned}
$$

Equality holds when $a = b = c = 1$, since then $a^2 + b^2 + c^2 = 3$ and the expression attains the value $\frac{3}{2}$. This gives the minimum value of the given sum, so $C = \frac{3}{2}$ is the largest constant for which the inequality always holds.

Therefore, the answer is $C = \frac{3}{2}$.

