For any real numbers $a_{1}, a_{2}, \ldots, a_{n}$ and $b_{1}, b_{2}, \ldots, b_{n}$

$$
\begin{gathered}
\left(a_{1}^{2}+a_{2}^{2}+\ldots+a_{n}^{2}\right)\left(b_{1}^{2}+b_{2}^{2}+\ldots+b_{n}^{2}\right) \geq \\
\geq\left(a_{1} b_{1}+a_{2} b_{2}+\ldots+a_{n} b_{n}\right)^{2}
\end{gathered}
$$

with equality if and only if $a_{i}$ and $b_{i}$ are proportional, $i=1,2, \ldots, n$.

solution:
By the well-known inequalities:
$$
x^{2}+y^{2} \geq 2 x y \quad \text { and } \quad 3\left(x^{2}+y^{2}+z^{2}\right) \geq(x+y+z)^{2}
$$
we obtain
$$
\begin{align*}
& \frac{b^{2}+c^{2}}{a}+\frac{c^{2}+a^{2}}{b}+\frac{a^{2}+b^{2}}{c} \\
& \quad \geq \frac{2 b c}{a}+\frac{2 c a}{b}+\frac{2 a b}{c}=\frac{2\left((b c)^{2}+(c a)^{2}+(a b)^{2}\right)}{a b c} \geq \frac{2(b c+c a+a b)^{2}}{3 a b c} \tag{1}
\end{align*}
$$

We have
$$
(a b+b c+c a)^{2} \geq 3((a b)(b c)+(b c)(c a)+(c a)(a b))=3 a b c(a+b+c)
i.e.
\begin{equation*}
a b+b c+c a \geq \sqrt{3 a b c(a+b+c)} \tag{2}
\end{equation*}
$$

Also
$$
4 a b c=a+b+c+1 \geq 4 \sqrt[4]{a b c}
i.e.
\begin{equation*}
a b c \geq 1 \tag{3}
\end{equation*}
$$

Therefore
$$
\begin{equation*}
a+b+c=4 a b c-1=3 a b c+a b c-1 \stackrel{(3)}{\geq} 3 a b c \tag{4}
\end{equation*}
$$

By (1), (2) and (4) we obtain
$$
\begin{aligned}
& \frac{b^{2}+c^{2}}{a}+\frac{c^{2}+a^{2}}{b}+\frac{a^{2}+b^{2}}{c} \\
& \geq \frac{2(b c+c a+a b)^{2}}{3 a b c} \geq \frac{2(a b+b c+c a) \sqrt{3 a b c(a+b+c)}}{3 a b c} \\
& \geq \frac{2(a b+b c+c a) \sqrt{(3 a b c)^{2}}}{3 a b c}=2(a b+b c+c a) .
\end{aligned}
$$

Equality holds when $a = b = c = 1$, since then $a + b + c + 1 = 4$ and $4abc = 4$, so the constraint is satisfied, and the inequality becomes equality. This gives the minimum value of the left side, so $C = 2$ is the largest constant for which the inequality always holds.

Therefore, the answer is $C = 2$.

Applying the Cauchy-Schwarz Inequality gives

$$
\begin{equation*}
3\left(a^{2}+b^{2}+c^{2}\right) \geq 3(a+b+c)^{2} \tag{1}
\end{equation*}
$$

and

$$
\begin{equation*}
\left(a^{2}+b^{2}+c^{2}\right)^{2} \leq(a+b+c)\left(a^{3}+b^{3}+c^{3}\right) \tag{2}
\end{equation*}
$$

These two inequalities combined yield

$$
\begin{align*}
a^{3}+b^{3}+c^{3} & \geq \frac{\left(a^{2}+b^{2}+c^{2}\right)(a+b+c)}{3} \\
& =\frac{\left(a^{2}+b^{2}+c^{2}\right)[(b+c)+(a+c)+(a+b)]}{6} \\
& \geq \frac{(a \sqrt{b+c}+b \sqrt{a+c}+c \sqrt{a+b})^{2}}{6} \tag{3}
\end{align*}
$$

Using the AM-GM Inequality we obtain

$$
\begin{aligned}
a \sqrt{b+c}+b \sqrt{a+c}+c \sqrt{a+b} & \geq 3 \sqrt[3]{a b c(\sqrt{(a+b)(b+c)(c+a)})} \\
& \geq 3 \sqrt[3]{a b c \sqrt{8 a b c}}=3 \sqrt[3]{8}=6
\end{aligned}
$$

Thus

$$
\begin{equation*}
(a \sqrt{b+c}+b \sqrt{a+c}+c \sqrt{a+b})^{2} \geq 6(a \sqrt{b+c}+b \sqrt{a+c}+c \sqrt{a+b}) \tag{4}
\end{equation*}
$$

The desired inequality follows from (3) and (4).

Equality holds when $a = b = c = \sqrt[3]{2}$, since $abc = 2$ and all terms are equal, so both sides of the inequality are equal. This gives the minimum value of $C$ for which the inequality always holds, which is $C = 1$.

Therefore, the answer is $C = 1$.

theorems.Theorem:
If $a_{1} \leq a_{2} \leq \ldots \leq a_{n}$ and $b_{1}, b_{2}, \ldots, b_{n}$ are real numbers, then
1)If $b_{1} \leq b_{2} \leq \ldots \leq b_{n}$ then $\sum_{i=1}^{n} a_{i} b_{i} \geq \frac{1}{n}\left(\sum_{i=1}^{n} a_{i}\right) \cdot\left(\sum_{i=1}^{n} b_{i}\right)$;
2)If $b_{1} \geq b_{2} \geq \ldots \geq b_{n}$ then $\sum_{i=1}^{n} a_{i} b_{i} \leq \frac{1}{n}\left(\sum_{i=1}^{n} a_{i}\right) \cdot\left(\sum_{i=1}^{n} b_{i}\right)$;

For any real numbers $a_{1}, a_{2}, \ldots, a_{n}$ and $b_{1}, b_{2}, \ldots, b_{n}$

$$
\begin{gathered}
\left(a_{1}^{2}+a_{2}^{2}+\ldots+a_{n}^{2}\right)\left(b_{1}^{2}+b_{2}^{2}+\ldots+b_{n}^{2}\right) \geq \\
\geq\left(a_{1} b_{1}+a_{2} b_{2}+\ldots+a_{n} b_{n}\right)^{2}
\end{gathered}
$$

with equality if and only if $a_{i}$ and $b_{i}$ are proportional, $i=1,2, \ldots, n$.

solution:
Let $p=x+y+z, q=x y+y z+z x, r=x y z$. Clearly $p, q, r \geq 0$.
Then $x^{2}+y^{2}+z^{2}=p^{2}-2 q$, and the constraint becomes
$$
\begin{equation*}
p^{2}-2 q=1 \tag{1}
\end{equation*}
$$

We can easily show that
$$
(1-x y)(1-y z)(1-z x)=1-q+p r-r^{2}
$$

Now the given inequality becomes
$$
\begin{equation*}
1-q+p r-r^{2} \geq \frac{8}{27} \tag{2}
\end{equation*}
$$

By $N_{1}: p^{3}-4 p q+9 r \geq 0$ and (1), we have
$$
\begin{align*}
& p\left(p^{2}-4 q\right)+9 r \geq 0 \\
& \quad \Leftrightarrow \quad p(1-2 q)+9 r \geq 0 \\
& \quad \Leftrightarrow \quad 9 r \geq p(2 q-1) \tag{3}
\end{align*}
$$

By $N_{4}: p^{2} \geq 3 q$ and $p^{2}-2 q=1$ we obtain
$$
\begin{equation*}
2 q+1 \geq 3 q, \quad \text { i.e. } \quad q \leq 1 \tag{4}
\end{equation*}
$$

From (4) and $N_{3}: p q-9 r \geq 0$ we obtain
$$
p \geq p q \geq 9 r \quad \Leftrightarrow \quad 9 p-9 r \geq 8 p \quad \Leftrightarrow \quad p-r \geq \frac{8}{9} p
$$
from which we deduce
$$
\begin{equation*}
r(p-r) \geq \frac{8}{9} p r \stackrel{(3)}{\geq} \frac{8}{9} p \frac{p(2 q-1)}{9}=\frac{8 p^{2}(2 q-1)}{81} \stackrel{(1)}{=} \frac{8(2 q+1)(2 q-1)}{81} \tag{5}
\end{equation*}
$$

Now we have
$$
\begin{equation*}
1-q+p r-r^{2}=1-q+r(p-r) \geq 1-q+\frac{8(2 q+1)(2 q-1)}{81} \tag{6}
\end{equation*}
$$

By (2) and (6), we have that it suffices to show that
$$
1-q+\frac{8(2 q+1)(2 q-1)}{81} \geq \frac{8}{27}
$$
which is equivalent to
$$
(1-q)(49-32 q) \geq 0
$$
which clearly holds, due to (4).

Equality holds when $x = y = z = \frac{1}{\sqrt{3}}$, since then $x^2 + y^2 + z^2 = 1$ and $xy = yz = zx = \frac{1}{3}$, so $(1 - xy)^3 = (1 - \frac{1}{3})^3 = (\frac{2}{3})^3 = \frac{8}{27}$. This gives the minimum value of the expression, so $C = \frac{8}{27}$ is the largest constant for which the inequality always holds.

Therefore, the answer is $C = \frac{8}{27}$.

We obtain that
$$
\begin{equation*}
\sqrt[3]{a b+b c}+\sqrt[3]{b c+c a}+\sqrt[3]{c a+a b} \leq \frac{1}{4}\left(\frac{2(a b+b c+c a)+48}{3}\right) \tag{1}
\end{equation*}
$$

At the point of incidence $a=b=c=2$ we have $a^{2}+b^{2}+c^{2}=12$.
Therefore by $A M \geq G M$ we have
$$
\begin{align*}
\sqrt[3]{\frac{9}{4}\left(a^{2}+b^{2}+c^{2}\right)} & =\sqrt[3]{\frac{9\left(a^{2}+b^{2}+c^{2}\right) \cdot 12 \cdot 12}{4 \cdot 12 \cdot 12}}=\frac{1}{4} \sqrt[3]{\left(a^{2}+b^{2}+c^{2}\right) \cdot 12 \cdot 12} \\
& \leq \frac{1}{4}\left(\frac{a^{2}+b^{2}+c^{2}+24}{3}\right) \tag{2}
\end{align*}
$$

By (1) and (2) we obtain
$$
\begin{aligned}
& \sqrt[3]{a b+b c}+\sqrt[3]{b c+c a}+\sqrt[3]{c a+a b}+\sqrt[3]{\frac{9}{2}\left(a^{2}+b^{2}+c^{2}\right)} \\
& \quad \leq \frac{1}{4}\left(\frac{2(a b+b c+c a)+48}{3}\right)+\frac{1}{4}\left(\frac{a^{2}+b^{2}+c^{2}+24}{3}\right) \\
& \quad=\frac{1}{12}\left((a+b+c)^{2}+72\right)=\frac{6^{2}+72}{12}=9
\end{aligned}
$$
as required.

Equality occurs if and only if $a = b = c = 2$, since in this case all the inequalities used above become equalities. This gives the maximum value of the left-hand side, so the minimal constant $C$ for which the inequality always holds is $C = 9$.

Therefore, the answer is $C = 9$.

