$$

Also
$$
\begin{equation*}
t_{a}^{2}+t_{b}^{2}+t_{c}^{2}=\frac{3}{4}\left(a^{2}+b^{2}+c^{2}\right) \tag{2}
\end{equation*}
$$

From (1) and (2) we get
$\left(a t_{a}+b t_{b}+c t_{c}\right)^{2} \leq \frac{3}{4}\left(a^{2}+b^{2}+c^{2}\right)^{2} \quad$ i.e. $\quad a t_{a}+b t_{b}+c t_{c} \leq \frac{\sqrt{3}}{2}\left(a^{2}+b^{2}+c^{2}\right)$,
as required.

Equality in the Cauchy-Schwarz inequality holds when $\frac{a}{t_a} = \frac{b}{t_b} = \frac{c}{t_c}$, which occurs when the triangle is equilateral. In this case, the value $C = \frac{\sqrt{3}}{2}$ is the smallest possible constant for which the inequality always holds.

Therefore, the answer is $C = \frac{\sqrt{3}}{2}$.

