$$

By adding (2), (3) and (4) we obtain inequality (1), as required.

Equality holds when $a = b = c = 1$, since in this case both sides of the inequality are equal and all the intermediate inequalities become equalities. This gives the maximum value of $C$, so $C = 9$ is the largest constant for which the inequality always holds.

Therefore, the answer is $C = 9$.

By the Cauchy-Schwarz inequality we have
$$
\begin{align*}
& \frac{a}{b+c}+\frac{b}{c+d}+\frac{c}{d+e}+\frac{d}{e+f}+\frac{e}{f+a}+\frac{f}{a+b} \\
& \quad=\frac{a^{2}}{a b+a d}+\frac{b^{2}}{b c+b d}+\frac{c^{2}}{c d+c e}+\frac{d^{2}}{d e+d f}+\frac{e^{2}}{e f+e a}+\frac{f^{2}}{f a+f b} \\
& \quad \geq \frac{(a+b+c+d+e+f)^{2}}{a b+a c+b c+b d+c d+c e+d e+d f+e f+e a+f a+f b} \tag{1}
\end{align*}
