theorems.Theorem:
For any real numbers $a_{1}, a_{2}, \ldots, a_{n}$ and $b_{1}, b_{2}, \ldots, b_{n}$

$$
\begin{gathered}
\left(a_{1}^{2}+a_{2}^{2}+\ldots+a_{n}^{2}\right)\left(b_{1}^{2}+b_{2}^{2}+\ldots+b_{n}^{2}\right) \geq \\
\geq\left(a_{1} b_{1}+a_{2} b_{2}+\ldots+a_{n} b_{n}\right)^{2}
\end{gathered}
$$

with equality if and only if $a_{i}$ and $b_{i}$ are proportional, $i=1,2, \ldots, n$.

solution:
Without loss of generality we may assume that $a \geq b \geq c \geq d$. Then clearly $a^{2} \geq b^{2} \geq c^{2} \geq d^{2}$.

We'll use Chebishev's inequality, i.e. we have
$$
\begin{aligned}
& (a+b+c)\left(a^{2}+b^{2}+c^{2}\right) \leq 3\left(a^{3}+b^{3}+c^{3}\right) \\
& \quad \Leftrightarrow \quad \frac{a^{3}+b^{3}+c^{3}}{a+b+c} \geq \frac{a^{2}+b^{2}+c^{2}}{3}
\end{aligned}
$$

Similarly we get
$$
\begin{aligned}
& \frac{a^{3}+b^{3}+d^{3}}{a+b+d} \geq \frac{a^{2}+b^{2}+d^{2}}{3}, \quad \frac{a^{3}+c^{3}+d^{3}}{a+c+d} \geq \frac{a^{2}+c^{2}+d^{2}}{3} \\
& \frac{b^{3}+c^{3}+d^{3}}{b+c+d} \geq \frac{b^{2}+c^{2}+d^{2}}{3}
\end{aligned}
$$

After adding these inequalities we get the required inequality. Therefore, the answer is (B) $\geq$.

Using $A M \geq G M$ we get
$$
\frac{a^{3}}{b^{2}}+a \geq 2 \sqrt{\frac{a^{3}}{b^{2}} \cdot a}=2 \frac{a^{2}}{b}
$$

Analogously we have
$$
\frac{b^{3}}{c^{2}}+b \geq 2 \frac{b^{2}}{c} \quad \text { and } \quad \frac{c^{3}}{a^{2}}+c \geq 2 \frac{c^{2}}{a}
$$

Adding these three inequalities we obtain
$$
\begin{equation*}
\frac{a^{3}}{b^{2}}+\frac{b^{3}}{c^{2}}+\frac{c^{3}}{a^{2}}+(a+b+c) \geq 2\left(\frac{a^{2}}{b}+\frac{b^{2}}{c}+\frac{c^{2}}{a}\right) \tag{1}
\end{equation*}
$$

We have that
$$
\begin{equation*}
\frac{a^{2}}{b}+\frac{b^{2}}{c}+\frac{c^{2}}{a} \geq a+b+c \tag{2}
\end{equation*}
$$

Now using (1) and (2) we obtain
$\frac{a^{3}}{b^{2}}+\frac{b^{3}}{c^{2}}+\frac{c^{3}}{a^{2}}+(a+b+c) \geq 2\left(\frac{a^{2}}{b}+\frac{b^{2}}{c}+\frac{c^{2}}{a}\right) \geq \frac{a^{2}}{b}+\frac{b^{2}}{c}+\frac{c^{2}}{a}+(a+b+c)$,
and equality holds iff $a=b=c$. Therefore, the answer is (B) $\geq$.

We have
$$
1+x^{2}=x y+y z+z x+x^{2}=(x+y)(x+z)
$$

Analogously we obtain
$$
1+y^{2}=(y+x)(y+z) \text { and } 1+z^{2}=(z+x)(z+y)
$$

Therefore
$$
\begin{align*}
\frac{x}{1+x^{2}}+\frac{y}{1+y^{2}}+\frac{z}{1+z^{2}} & =\frac{x}{(x+y)(x+z)}+\frac{y}{(y+x)(y+z)}+\frac{z}{(z+x)(z+y)} \\
& =\frac{x(y+z)+y(x+z)+z(x+y)}{(x+y)(y+z)(z+x)} \\
& =\frac{2}{(x+y)(y+z)(z+x)} \tag{1}
\end{align*}
$$

It is easy to show that
$$
\begin{equation*}
(x+y)(y+z)(z+x)=x+y+z-x y z \tag{2}
\end{equation*}
$$

Due to the well-known inequality $(x+y+z)^{2} \geq 3(x y+y z+z x)$ we obtain
$$
\begin{equation*}
(x+y+z)^{2} \geq 3(x y+y z+z x)=3, \text { i.e. } \quad x+y+z \geq \sqrt{3} \tag{3}
\end{equation*}
$$

Applying $A M \geq G M$ it follows that
$$
x y+y z+z x \geq 3 \sqrt[3]{(x y z)^{2}}
$$
i.e.
$$
\begin{equation*}
\frac{1}{27} \geq(x y z)^{2} \quad \Leftrightarrow \quad \frac{1}{3 \sqrt{3}} \geq x y z \tag{4}
\end{equation*}
$$

Using (3) and (4) we obtain
$$
\begin{equation*}
x+y+z-x y z \geq \sqrt{3}-\frac{1}{3 \sqrt{3}}=\frac{8}{3 \sqrt{3}} \tag{5}
\end{equation*}
$$

Finally using (1), (2) and (5) we get
$$
\frac{x}{1+x^{2}}+\frac{y}{1+y^{2}}+\frac{z}{1+z^{2}}=\frac{2}{(x+y)(y+z)(z+x)}=\frac{2}{x+y+z-x y z} \leq \frac{3 \sqrt{3}}{4}
$$

Equality occurs iff $x=y=z=\frac{1}{\sqrt{3}}$. Therefore, the answer is (A) $\leq$.

Since $1-b c=a c+a b, 1-c a=a b+b c$ and $1-a b=a c+b c$, the given inequality can be rewritten as
$$
\frac{1}{a(4 a+b+c)}+\frac{1}{b(4 b+c+a)}+\frac{1}{c(4 c+a+b)} \geq \frac{3}{2} .
$$

By the Cauchy-Schwarz inequality we get
$$
\begin{aligned}
& \left(\frac{1}{a(4 a+b+c)}+\frac{1}{b(4 b+c+a)}+\frac{1}{c(4 c+a+b)}\right) \\
& \quad \times\left(\frac{4 a+b+c}{a}+\frac{4 b+c+a}{b}+\frac{4 c+a+b}{c}\right) \\
& \quad \geq\left(\frac{1}{a}+\frac{1}{b}+\frac{1}{c}\right)^{2}=\frac{1}{a^{2} b^{2} c^{2}}
\end{aligned}
$$

So it suffices to prove that
$$
\begin{equation*}
\frac{2}{3 a^{2} b^{2} c^{2}} \geq \frac{4 a+b+c}{a}+\frac{4 b+c+a}{b}+\frac{4 c+a+b}{c} \tag{1}
\end{equation*}
$$

We have
$$
\begin{aligned}
& \frac{4 a+b+c}{a}+\frac{4 b+c+a}{b}+\frac{4 c+a+b}{c} \\
& \quad=9+\frac{a+b+c}{a}+\frac{b+c+a}{b}+\frac{c+a+b}{c} \\
& \quad=9+(a+b+c)\left(\frac{1}{a}+\frac{1}{b}+\frac{1}{c}\right) \\
& \quad=9+\frac{(a+b+c)(a b+b c+c a)}{a b c} \\
& \quad=9+\frac{a+b+c}{a b c}
\end{aligned}
$$
so inequality (1) becomes
$$
\begin{equation*}
\frac{2}{3 a^{2} b^{2} c^{2}} \geq 9+\frac{a+b+c}{a b c}, \quad \text { i.e. } \quad 27 a^{2} b^{2} c^{2}+3 a b c(a+b+c) \leq 2 \tag{2}
\end{equation*}
$$

By $A M \geq G M$ we have
$$
\begin{equation*}
1=a b+b c+c a \geq 3 \sqrt[3]{a^{2} b^{2} c^{2}}, \quad \text { i.e. } \quad 27 a^{2} b^{2} c^{2} \leq 1 \tag{3}
\end{equation*}
$$

By the well-known inequality $(x+y+z)^{2} \geq 3(x y+y z+z x)$ we get
$$
\begin{equation*}
3 a b c(a+b+c) \leq(a b+b c+c a)^{2}=1 \tag{4}
\end{equation*}
$$

Finally by (3) and (4) we get inequality (2), as required.
Equality occurs iff $a=b=c=\frac{1}{\sqrt{3}}$. Therefore, the answer is (B) $\geq$.

Let
$A=b+2 c+3 d, \quad B=c+2 d+3 a, \quad C=d+2 a+3 b, \quad D=a+2 b+3 c$.
By the Cauchy-Schwarz inequality we have
$$
\begin{align*}
\left(\frac{a}{A}+\frac{b}{B}\right. & \left.+\frac{c}{C}+\frac{d}{D}\right)(a A+b B+c C+d D) \geq(a+b+c+d)^{2} \\
\Leftrightarrow \quad & \frac{a}{b+2 c+3 d}+\frac{b}{c+2 d+3 a}+\frac{c}{d+2 a+3 b}+\frac{d}{a+2 b+3 c} \\
& \geq \frac{(a+b+c+d)^{2}}{a A+b B+c C+d D} \tag{1}
\end{align*}
$$

Furthermore
$$
a A+b B+c C+d D=4(a b+a c+a d+b c+b d+c d)
$$
and (1) becomes
$$
\begin{aligned}
& \frac{a}{b+2 c+3 d}+\frac{b}{c+2 d+3 a}+\frac{c}{d+2 a+3 b}+\frac{d}{a+2 b+3 c} \\
& \geq \frac{(a+b+c+d)^{2}}{4(a b+a c+a d+b c+b d+c d)}
\end{aligned}
$$

So it suffices to prove that
$$
\frac{(a+b+c+d)^{2}}{4(a b+a c+a d+b c+b d+c d)} \geq \frac{2}{3}
$$
i.e.
$$
\begin{equation*}
3(a+b+c+d)^{2} \geq 8(a b+a c+a d+b c+b d+c d) \tag{2}
\end{equation*}
$$

We'll use Maclaurin's theorem.
We have
$$
\begin{aligned}
& p_{2}=\frac{c_{2}}{6}=\frac{a b+a c+a d+b c+b d+c d}{6}, \quad \text { i.e. } \\
& a b+a c+a d+b c+b d+c d=6 p_{2}
\end{aligned}
$$
and
$$
p_{1}=\frac{c_{1}}{4}=\frac{a+b+c+d}{4}, \quad \text { i.e. } \quad a+b+c+d=4 p_{1}
$$

Now inequality (2) is equivalent to $48 p_{1}^{2} \geq 48 p_{2}$, i.e. $p_{1} \geq p_{2}^{1 / 2}$, which is true due to Maclaurin's theorem. Therefore, the answer is (B) $\geq$.

The given inequality is equivalent to
$$
\left(\frac{x-1}{x}\right)\left(\frac{y-1}{y}\right)\left(\frac{z-1}{z}\right) \geq \frac{8}{x y z}
$$
or
$$
\begin{equation*}
\left(1-\frac{1}{x}\right)\left(1-\frac{1}{y}\right)\left(1-\frac{1}{z}\right) \geq \frac{8}{x y z}
\end{equation*}
$$

From the initial condition and $A M \geq G M$ we have
$$
1-\frac{1}{x}=\frac{1}{y}+\frac{1}{z} \geq 2 \sqrt{\frac{1}{y} \frac{1}{z}}=\frac{2}{\sqrt{y z}}
$$

Analogously we obtain $1-\frac{1}{y} \geq \frac{2}{\sqrt{z x}}$ and $1-\frac{1}{z} \geq \frac{2}{\sqrt{x y}}$.
If we multiply the last three inequalities we get inequality (2.5), as required. Equality holds if and only if $x=y=z=3$. Therefore, the answer is (B) $\geq$.

theorems.Theorem:
For any real numbers $a_{1}, a_{2}, \ldots, a_{n}$ and $b_{1}, b_{2}, \ldots, b_{n}$

$$
\begin{gathered}
\left(a_{1}^{2}+a_{2}^{2}+\ldots+a_{n}^{2}\right)\left(b_{1}^{2}+b_{2}^{2}+\ldots+b_{n}^{2}\right) \geq \\
\geq\left(a_{1} b_{1}+a_{2} b_{2}+\ldots+a_{n} b_{n}\right)^{2}
\end{gathered}
$$

with equality if and only if $a_{i}$ and $b_{i}$ are proportional, $i=1,2, \ldots, n$.

solution:
From $A M \geq G M$ we have
$$
\begin{align*}
\frac{2}{9} \sum_{1 \leq i<j \leq 4} \frac{1}{\sqrt{\left(s-a_{i}\right)\left(s-a_{j}\right)}} & \geq \frac{2}{9} \cdot 2 \sum_{1 \leq i<j \leq 4} \frac{1}{\left(s-a_{i}\right)+\left(s-a_{j}\right)} \\
& =\frac{4}{9} \sum_{1 \leq i<j \leq 4} \frac{1}{a_{i}+a_{j}} \tag{1}
\end{align*}
$$

Let $a_{1}=a, a_{2}=b, a_{3}=c, a_{4}=d$.
We'll show that
$$
\begin{aligned}
& \frac{2}{9}\left(\frac{1}{a+b}+\frac{1}{a+c}+\frac{1}{a+d}+\frac{1}{b+c}+\frac{1}{b+d}+\frac{1}{c+d}\right) \\
& \quad \geq \frac{1}{3 a+b+c+d}+\frac{1}{a+3 b+c+d}+\frac{1}{a+b+3 c+d}+\frac{1}{a+b+c+3 d}
\end{aligned}
$$

From $A M \geq H M$ we deduce
$$
\left(\frac{1}{a+b}+\frac{1}{a+c}+\frac{1}{a+d}\right)((a+b)+(a+c)+(a+d)) \geq 9
$$
i.e.
$$
\frac{1}{9}\left(\frac{1}{a+b}+\frac{1}{a+c}+\frac{1}{a+d}\right) \geq \frac{1}{3 a+b+c+d}
$$

Similarly we obtain
$$
\begin{aligned}
& \frac{1}{9}\left(\frac{1}{a+b}+\frac{1}{b+c}+\frac{1}{b+d}\right) \geq \frac{1}{a+3 b+c+d} \\
& \frac{1}{9}\left(\frac{1}{a+c}+\frac{1}{b+c}+\frac{1}{c+d}\right) \geq \frac{1}{a+b+3 c+d} \\
& \frac{1}{9}\left(\frac{1}{a+d}+\frac{1}{b+d}+\frac{1}{c+d}\right) \geq \frac{1}{a+b+c+3 d}
\end{aligned}
$$

Adding these inequalities we get
$$
\begin{aligned}
& \frac{2}{9}\left(\frac{1}{a+b}+\frac{1}{a+c}+\frac{1}{a+d}+\frac{1}{b+c}+\frac{1}{b+d}+\frac{1}{c+d}\right) \\
& \quad \geq \frac{1}{3 a+b+c+d}+\frac{1}{a+3 b+c+d}+\frac{1}{a+b+3 c+d}+\frac{1}{a+b+c+3 d} \\
& \quad=\frac{1}{2}\left(\frac{1}{s+a}+\frac{1}{s+b}+\frac{1}{s+c}+\frac{1}{s+d}\right)
\end{aligned}
$$
i.e.
$$
\begin{equation*}
\frac{4}{9} \sum_{1 \leq i<j \leq 4} \frac{1}{a_{i}+a_{j}} \geq \sum_{i=1}^{4} \frac{1}{s+a_{i}} \tag{2}
\end{equation*}
$$

From (1) and (2) we obtain the given inequality.
Equality holds iff $a=b=c=d$. Therefore, the answer is (A) $\leq$.

Without loss of generality we may assume that $x \geq y \geq z$.
Then
$$
\frac{x}{y+z} \geq \frac{y}{z+x} \geq \frac{z}{x+y} \quad \text { and } \quad x^{\alpha-1} \geq y^{\alpha-1} \geq z^{\alpha-1}
$$

Applying Chebishev's inequality we have
$$
\begin{equation*}
\left(x^{\alpha-1}+y^{\alpha-1}+z^{\alpha-1}\right)\left(\frac{x}{y+z}+\frac{y}{z+x}+\frac{z}{x+y}\right) \leq 3\left(\frac{x^{\alpha}}{y+z}+\frac{y^{\alpha}}{z+x}+\frac{z^{\alpha}}{x+y}\right) \tag{1}
\end{equation*}
$$

Recalling $A M \geq G M$ we get
$$
\begin{equation*}
x^{\alpha-1}+y^{\alpha-1}+z^{\alpha-1} \geq 3 \sqrt[3]{(x y z)^{\alpha-1}}=3 \tag{2}
\end{equation*}
$$

Nesbitt's inequality gives us
$$
\begin{equation*}
\frac{x}{y+z}+\frac{y}{z+x}+\frac{z}{x+y} \geq \frac{3}{2} \tag{3}
\end{equation*}
$$

Finally using (1), (2) and (3) we obtain
$$
\begin{aligned}
3\left(\frac{x^{\alpha}}{y+z}+\frac{y^{\alpha}}{z+x}+\frac{z^{\alpha}}{x+y}\right) & \geq\left(x^{\alpha-1}+y^{\alpha-1}+z^{\alpha-1}\right)\left(\frac{x}{y+z}+\frac{y}{z+x}+\frac{z}{x+y}\right) \\
& =3 \cdot \frac{3}{2}
\end{aligned}
$$
i.e.
$$
\frac{x^{\alpha}}{y+z}+\frac{y^{\alpha}}{z+x}+\frac{z^{\alpha}}{x+y} \geq \frac{3}{2}
$$ Therefore, the answer is (B) $\geq$.

We have $P=\frac{a d_{1}+b d_{2}+c d_{3}}{2}$, i.e.
$$
\begin{equation*}
P^{2}=\frac{1}{4}\left(a d_{1}+b d_{2}+c d_{3}\right)^{2} \tag{1}
\end{equation*}
$$

By the Cauchy-Schwarz inequality we have
$$
\begin{equation*}
\left(a d_{1}+b d_{2}+c d_{3}\right)^{2} \leq\left(a^{2}+b^{2}+c^{2}\right)\left(d_{1}^{2}+d_{2}^{2}+d_{3}^{2}\right) \tag{2}
\end{equation*}
$$

Also
$$
\begin{equation*}
a^{2}+b^{2}+c^{2} \leq 9 R^{2} \tag{3}
\end{equation*}
$$

Finally by (1), (2) and (3) we obtain the required inequality.
Equality holds iff the triangle is equilateral and the given point is the center of the triangle. Therefore, the answer is (B) $\geq$.

Without loss of generality we can assume that $a \geq b \geq c$.
Then clearly
$$
\frac{1}{b+c} \geq \frac{1}{c+a} \geq \frac{1}{a+b}
$$

By Chebishev's inequality we have
$$
\begin{equation*}
\frac{2 a^{2}}{b+c}+\frac{2 b^{2}}{c+a}+\frac{2 c^{2}}{a+b} \geq \frac{2}{3}\left(a^{2}+b^{2}+c^{2}\right)\left(\frac{1}{b+c}+\frac{1}{c+a}+\frac{1}{a+b}\right) \tag{1}
\end{equation*}
$$

Applying $Q M \geq A M$ we deduce
$$
\sqrt{\frac{a^{2}+b^{2}+c^{2}}{3}} \geq \frac{a+b+c}{3}, \quad \text { i.e. } \quad \frac{a^{2}+b^{2}+c^{2}}{3} \geq\left(\frac{a+b+c}{3}\right)^{2}
$$

By (1) and the previous inequality it follows that
$$
\begin{equation*}
\frac{2 a^{2}}{b+c}+\frac{2 b^{2}}{c+a}+\frac{2 c^{2}}{a+b} \geq \frac{2}{9}(a+b+c)^{2}\left(\frac{1}{b+c}+\frac{1}{c+a}+\frac{1}{a+b}\right) \tag{2}
\end{equation*}
$$

Applying $A M \geq H M$ we deduce
$$
\frac{1}{b+c}+\frac{1}{c+a}+\frac{1}{a+b} \geq \frac{9}{(b+c)+(c+a)+(a+b)}=\frac{9}{2(a+b+c)}
$$

Finally from the previous inequality and (2), we get required result. Therefore, the answer is (B) $\geq$.

theorems.Theorem:
For any real numbers $a_{1}, a_{2}, \ldots, a_{n}$ and $b_{1}, b_{2}, \ldots, b_{n}$

$$
\begin{gathered}
\left(a_{1}^{2}+a_{2}^{2}+\ldots+a_{n}^{2}\right)\left(b_{1}^{2}+b_{2}^{2}+\ldots+b_{n}^{2}\right) \geq \\
\geq\left(a_{1} b_{1}+a_{2} b_{2}+\ldots+a_{n} b_{n}\right)^{2}
\end{gathered}
$$

with equality if and only if $a_{i}$ and $b_{i}$ are proportional, $i=1,2, \ldots, n$.

Let $f:[a, b] \rightarrow \mathbb{R}$ and $g:[a, b] \rightarrow \mathbb{R}$ be functions such that:
(i) $f$ and $g$ are continuous on $[a, b]$ and $f(a)=g(a)$;
(ii) $f$ and $g$ are differentiable on $(a, b)$;
(iii) $f^{\prime}(x)>g^{\prime}(x)$, for all $x \in(a, b)$.

Then, for all $x \in(a, b)$, we have $f(x)>g(x)$.

For any real numbers $a_{1}, a_{2}, \ldots, a_{n}$ and $b_{1}, b_{2}, \ldots, b_{n}$

$$
\begin{gathered}
\left(a_{1}^{2}+a_{2}^{2}+\ldots+a_{n}^{2}\right)\left(b_{1}^{2}+b_{2}^{2}+\ldots+b_{n}^{2}\right) \geq \\
\geq\left(a_{1} b_{1}+a_{2} b_{2}+\ldots+a_{n} b_{n}\right)^{2}
\end{gathered}
$$

with equality if and only if $a_{i}$ and $b_{i}$ are proportional, $i=1,2, \ldots, n$.

solution:
Without loss of generality we may assume $a \geq b \geq c$.
Let's denote $u=\frac{a+b}{2}$ and $v=\frac{a-b}{2}$.
We easily obtain $a=u+v$ and $b=u-v$.
We have $a b=u^{2}-v^{2} \geq c^{2}$ which implies $2 u^{2}-2 c^{2}-v^{2} \geq 0$.
Now we have
$$
\begin{align*}
a^{2} b^{2}+b^{2} c^{2}+c^{2} a^{2} & =c^{2}\left(a^{2}+b^{2}\right)+a^{2} b^{2}=c^{2}\left(2 u^{2}+2 v^{2}\right)+\left(u^{2}-v^{2}\right)^{2} \\
& =-v^{2}\left(2 u^{2}-2 c^{2}-v^{2}\right)+u^{4}+2 c^{2} u^{2} \leq u^{4}+2 c^{2} u^{2} \tag{1}
\end{align*}
$$

Also
$$
\begin{equation*}
a^{2}+b^{2}+c^{2}=2 u^{2}+2 v^{2}+c^{2} \geq 2 u^{2}+c^{2} \tag{2}
\end{equation*}
$$

We'll show that
$$
\begin{equation*}
2\left(u^{4}+2 c^{2} u^{2}\right)+3 \leq 3\left(2 u^{2}+c^{2}\right) \tag{3}
\end{equation*}
$$

From $a+b+c=3$ we have $c=3-2 u$.
Now inequality (3) is equivalent to
$$
\begin{aligned}
& 2 u^{4}+4(3-2 u)^{2} u^{2}+3 \leq 6 u^{2}+3(3-2 u)^{2} \\
& \quad \Leftrightarrow \quad 3 u^{4}-8 u^{3}+3 u^{2}+6 u-4 \leq 0 \quad \Leftrightarrow \quad(u-1)^{2}\left(3 u^{2}-2 u-4\right) \leq 0
\end{aligned}
$$

Since $2 u \leq 3$ we easily deduce that $3 u^{2}-2 u-4 \leq 0$. So inequality (3) holds.

Combining (1), (2) and (3) we obtain the required result.
Equality holds if and only if $a=b=c=1$. Therefore, the answer is (A) $\leq$.

Applying the Cauchy-Schwarz inequality for
$$
\begin{array}{lll}
a_{1}=\sqrt{b+c}, & a_{2}=\sqrt{c+a}, & a_{3}=\sqrt{a+b} \\
b_{1}=\frac{1}{\sqrt{b+c}}, & b_{2}=\frac{1}{\sqrt{c+a}}, & b_{3}=\frac{1}{\sqrt{a+b}}
\end{array}
$$
gives us
$$
((b+c)+(c+a)+(a+b))\left(\frac{1}{b+c}+\frac{1}{c+a}+\frac{1}{a+b}\right) \geq(1+1+1)^{2}=9
$$
i.e.
$$
\begin{aligned}
& 2(a+b+c)\left(\frac{1}{b+c}+\frac{1}{c+a}+\frac{1}{a+b}\right) \geq 9 \\
& \quad \Leftrightarrow \quad \frac{a+b+c}{b+c}+\frac{a+b+c}{c+a}+\frac{a+b+c}{a+b} \geq \frac{9}{2} \\
& \quad \Leftrightarrow \quad \frac{a}{b+c}+\frac{b}{c+a}+\frac{c}{a+b} \geq \frac{9}{2}-3=\frac{3}{2}
\end{aligned}
$$

Equality occurs iff $(b+c)^{2}=(c+a)^{2}=(a+b)^{2}$, i.e. iff $a=b=c$.2 We'll use Chebishev's inequality.
Assume that $a \geq b \geq c$; then $\frac{1}{b+c} \geq \frac{1}{c+a} \geq \frac{1}{a+b}$.
Now by Chebishev's inequality we get
$$
\begin{equation*}
(a+b+c)\left(\frac{1}{b+c}+\frac{1}{c+a}+\frac{1}{a+b}\right) \leq 3\left(\frac{a}{b+c}+\frac{b}{c+a}+\frac{c}{a+b}\right) \tag{1}
\end{equation*}
$$

Note that
$$
\begin{aligned}
(a+b+c)\left(\frac{1}{b+c}+\frac{1}{c+a}+\frac{1}{a+b}\right)= & \frac{1}{2}((b+c)+(c+a)+(a+b)) \\
& \times\left(\frac{1}{b+c}+\frac{1}{c+a}+\frac{1}{a+b}\right)
\end{aligned}
$$

Since $A M \geq H M$ (the same thing in this case with Cauchy-Schwarz) we have
$$
((b+c)+(c+a)+(a+b))\left(\frac{1}{b+c}+\frac{1}{c+a}+\frac{1}{a+b}\right) \geq 9
$$

Therefore
$$
\begin{equation*}
(a+b+c)\left(\frac{1}{b+c}+\frac{1}{c+a}+\frac{1}{a+b}\right) \geq \frac{9}{2} \tag{2}
\end{equation*}
$$

By (1) and (2) we obtain
$$
3\left(\frac{a}{b+c}+\frac{b}{c+a}+\frac{c}{a+b}\right) \geq \frac{9}{2}, \quad \text { i.e. } \quad \frac{a}{b+c}+\frac{b}{c+a}+\frac{c}{a+b} \geq \frac{3}{2}
$$

Equality occurs iff $a=b=c$. Therefore, the answer is (B) $\geq$.

Firstly it is easy to show that if there exists a triangle with lengths sides $a, b, c$ then there also exists a triangle with length $\operatorname{sides} \sqrt{a}, \sqrt{b}, \sqrt{c}$.

Furthermore
$$
(\sqrt{a}+\sqrt{b}+\sqrt{c})^{2}=a+b+c+2(\sqrt{a b}+\sqrt{b c}+\sqrt{c a}) \leq 3(a+b+c)
$$
i.e.
$$
\begin{equation*}
\frac{1}{\sqrt{a}+\sqrt{b}+\sqrt{c}} \geq \frac{\sqrt{a}+\sqrt{b}+\sqrt{c}}{3(a+b+c)} \tag{1}
\end{equation*}
$$

Applying $A M \geq H M$ we deduce
$$
\frac{3}{\frac{1}{\sqrt{a}+\sqrt{b}-\sqrt{c}}+\frac{1}{\sqrt{b}+\sqrt{c}-\sqrt{a}}+\frac{1}{\sqrt{c}+\sqrt{a}-\sqrt{b}}} \leq \frac{\sqrt{a}+\sqrt{b}+\sqrt{c}}{3}
$$
i.e.
$$
\begin{equation*}
\frac{1}{\sqrt{a}+\sqrt{b}-\sqrt{c}}+\frac{1}{\sqrt{b}+\sqrt{c}-\sqrt{a}}+\frac{1}{\sqrt{c}+\sqrt{a}-\sqrt{b}} \geq \frac{9}{\sqrt{a}+\sqrt{b}+\sqrt{c}} \tag{2}
\end{equation*}
$$

By (1) and (2) we get the required inequality. Therefore, the answer is (B) $\geq$.

After taking $a=x y, b=y z, c=z x$, inequality becomes
$$
\begin{equation*}
x^{2} y^{2}+y^{2} z^{2}+z^{2} x^{2}+2 \sqrt{3} x y z \leq 1 \tag{1}
\end{equation*}
$$
where $x, y, z$ are positive real numbers such that
$$
\begin{equation*}
x y+y z+z x=1 \tag{2}
\end{equation*}
$$

Inequality (1) is equivalent to
$$
(x y+y z+z x)^{2}+2 \sqrt{3} x y z \leq 1+2 x y z(x+y+z)
$$
or
$$
\begin{equation*}
\sqrt{3} \leq x+y+z \tag{3}
\end{equation*}
$$

By (2) and according to Case 3, we can take
$$
x=\tan \frac{\alpha}{2}, \quad y=\tan \frac{\beta}{2}, \quad z=\tan \frac{\gamma}{2}
$$
where $\alpha, \beta, \gamma \in(0, \pi)$ and $\alpha+\beta+\gamma=\pi$.
Then inequality (8.12) is equivalent to $\tan \frac{\alpha}{2}+\tan \frac{\beta}{2}+\tan \frac{\gamma}{2} \geq \sqrt{3}$, which is $N_{13}$. Therefore, the answer is (A) $\leq$.

By Minkowski's inequality we have
$$
\begin{aligned}
& \sqrt{a^{2}+(1-b)^{2}}+\sqrt{b^{2}+(1-c)^{2}}+\sqrt{c^{2}+(1-a)^{2}} \\
& \quad \geq \sqrt{(a+b+c)^{2}+(3-a-b-c)^{2}}=\sqrt{2\left(a+b+c-\frac{3}{2}\right)^{2}+\frac{9}{2}} \geq \frac{3 \sqrt{2}}{2}
\end{aligned}
$$ Therefore, the answer is (B) $\geq$.

theorems.Theorem:
For any real numbers $a_{1}, a_{2}, \ldots, a_{n}$ and $b_{1}, b_{2}, \ldots, b_{n}$

$$
\begin{gathered}
\left(a_{1}^{2}+a_{2}^{2}+\ldots+a_{n}^{2}\right)\left(b_{1}^{2}+b_{2}^{2}+\ldots+b_{n}^{2}\right) \geq \\
\geq\left(a_{1} b_{1}+a_{2} b_{2}+\ldots+a_{n} b_{n}\right)^{2}
\end{gathered}
$$

with equality if and only if $a_{i}$ and $b_{i}$ are proportional, $i=1,2, \ldots, n$.

solution:
Using the identity
$$
(a+b)(b+c)(c+a)=(a+b+c)(a b+b c+c a)-1
$$
the given inequality becomes
$$
a b+b c+c a+\frac{3}{a+b+c} \geq 4
$$

By $A M \geq G M$ we have
$a b+b c+c a+\frac{3}{a+b+c}=\frac{3(a b+b c+c a)}{3}+\frac{3}{a+b+c} \geq 4 \sqrt[4]{\frac{(a b+b c+c a)^{3}}{9(a+b+c)}}$.
So it's enough to show that
$$
\begin{equation*}
(a b+b c+c a)^{3} \geq 9(a+b+c) \tag{1}
\end{equation*}
$$

By $A M \geq G M$ and $a b c=1$ we get
$$
\begin{equation*}
a b+b c+c a \geq 3 \sqrt[3]{(a b c)^{2}}=3 \tag{2}
\end{equation*}
$$

Furthermore, since $(x+y+z)^{2} \geq 3(x y+y z+z x)$, we deduce
$$
\begin{equation*}
(a b+b c+c a)^{2} \geq 3((a b)(b c)+(b c)(c a)+(c a)(a b))=3(a+b+c) \tag{3}
\end{equation*}
$$

By (2) and (3) we obtain $(a b+b c+c a)^{3} \geq 9(a+b+c)$, i.e. (1) is true. Therefore, the answer is (B) $\geq$.

Let $p=a+b+c, q=a b+b c+c a, r=a b c$.
Since the given inequality is homogenous, without loss of generality we may assume that $p=1$.

We have
$$
\begin{aligned}
x^{4}(y+z)+y^{4}(z+x)+z^{4}(x+y) & =x^{3}(x y+x z)+y^{3}(y z+y x)+z^{3}(z x+z y) \\
& =x^{3}(q-y z)+y^{3}(q-z x)+z^{3}(q-x y) \\
& =q\left(x^{3}+y^{3}+z^{3}\right)-x y z\left(x^{2}+y^{2}+z^{2}\right) \\
& =q\left(p\left(p^{2}-3 q\right)+3 r\right)-r\left(p^{2}-2 q\right) \\
& =q(1-3 q+3 r)-r(1-2 q) \\
& =q(1-3 q)+r(5 q-1) .
\end{aligned}
$$

Now the given inequality becomes
$$
\begin{equation*}
q(1-3 q)+r(5 q-1) \leq \frac{1}{12} \tag{1}
\end{equation*}
$$

From $3 q \leq p^{2}$ it follows that
$$
\begin{equation*}
q \leq \frac{1}{3} \tag{2}
\end{equation*}
$$

If $q \leq \frac{1}{5}$ then $r(5 q-1) \leq 0$, so we have
$q(1-3 q)+r(5 q-1) \leq q(1-3 q)=\frac{1}{3}(1-3 q) \cdot 3 q \stackrel{G \leq A}{\leq} \frac{1}{3}\left(\frac{(1-3 q)+3 q}{2}\right)^{2}=\frac{1}{12}$,
i.e. inequality (1) holds.

Let
$$
\begin{equation*}
q>\frac{1}{5} \tag{3}
\end{equation*}
$$
i.e. let $q \in(1 / 5,1 / 3]$ and denote
$$
f(q)=q(1-3 q)+5 r q-r
$$

Then
$$
\begin{equation*}
f^{\prime}(q)=1-6 q+5 r \tag{4}
\end{equation*}
$$

Using $p q \geq 9 r$ , we get
$$
\begin{equation*}
q \geq 9 r \tag{5}
\end{equation*}
$$

Now according to (3), (4) and (5) we deduce
$$
f^{\prime}(q)=1-6 q+5 r \leq 1-6 q+\frac{5}{9} q=1-\frac{49}{9} q<1-\frac{49}{9} \cdot \frac{1}{5}<0
$$
i.e. $f$ is strictly decreasing on $q \in(1 / 5,1 / 3]$, so it follows that $f(q)<f\left(\frac{1}{5}\right)$, i.e. we deduce that
$$
q(1-3 q)+r(5 q-1)<\frac{1}{5}\left(1-\frac{3}{5}\right)+r\left(5 \frac{1}{5}-1\right)=\frac{2}{25}<\frac{1}{12}
$$
as required. Therefore, the answer is (A) $\leq$.

At the point of incidence $a=b=c=1$ we have $5 a^{2}+4(b+c)+3=16$. By the inequality $A M \geq G M$ we get
$$
\begin{aligned}
\sqrt[4]{5 a^{2}+4(b+c)+3} & =\frac{\sqrt[4]{\left(5 a^{2}+4(b+c)+3\right) \cdot 16^{3}}}{8} \\
& \leq \frac{1}{32}\left(5 a^{2}+4(b+c)+3+3 \cdot 16\right) \\
& =\frac{5 a^{2}+4(b+c)+51}{32}
\end{aligned}
$$

Similarly we obtain
$$
\sqrt[4]{5 b^{2}+4(c+a)+3} \leq \frac{5 b^{2}+4(c+a)+51}{32}
$$
and
$$
\sqrt[4]{5 c^{2}+4(a+b)+3} \leq \frac{5 c^{2}+4(a+b)+51}{32}
$$

Adding the last three inequalities gives us
$$
\begin{aligned}
& \sqrt[4]{5 a^{2}+4(b+c)+3}+\sqrt[4]{5 b^{2}+4(c+a)+3}+\sqrt[4]{5 c^{2}+4(a+b)+3} \\
& \quad \leq \frac{5\left(a^{2}+b^{2}+c^{2}\right)+8(a+b+c)+153}{32}
\end{aligned}
$$

Since $a^{2}+b^{2}+c^{2}=3$ we have $a+b+c \leq \sqrt{3\left(a^{2}+b^{2}+c^{2}\right)}=3$, and by the last inequality we obtain
$$
\begin{aligned}
& \sqrt[4]{5 a^{2}+4(b+c)+3}+\sqrt[4]{5 b^{2}+4(c+a)+3}+\sqrt[4]{5 c^{2}+4(a+b)+3} \\
& \quad \leq \frac{5 \cdot 3+8 \cdot 3+153}{32}=\frac{192}{32}=6
\end{aligned}
$$

Equality occurs if and only if $a=b=c=1$. Therefore, the answer is (A) $\leq$.

We'll use the Cauchy-Schwarz inequality, i.e.
$$
\begin{equation*}
\left(a_{1}^{2}+a_{2}^{2}+a_{3}^{2}\right)\left(b_{1}^{2}+b_{2}^{2}+b_{3}^{2}\right) \geq\left(a_{1} b_{1}+a_{2} b_{2}+a_{3} b_{3}\right)^{2} \tag{1}
\end{equation*}
$$

Let
$$
\begin{aligned}
& a_{1}=\sqrt{a^{2}\left(b^{2}+1\right)}, \quad a_{2}=\sqrt{b^{2}\left(c^{2}+1\right)}, \quad a_{3}=\sqrt{c^{2}\left(a^{2}+1\right)} \quad \text { and } \\
& b_{1}=\sqrt{\frac{a}{b^{2}+1}}, \quad b_{2}=\sqrt{\frac{b}{c^{2}+1}}, \quad b_{3}=\sqrt{\frac{c}{a^{2}+1}} .
\end{aligned}
$$

Then using (1) we get
$$
\begin{aligned}
& \left(a^{2}\left(b^{2}+1\right)+b^{2}\left(c^{2}+1\right)+c^{2}\left(a^{2}+1\right)\right)\left(\frac{a}{b^{2}+1}+\frac{b}{c^{2}+1}+\frac{c}{a^{2}+1}\right) \\
& \quad \geq(a \sqrt{a}+b \sqrt{b}+c \sqrt{c})^{2}
\end{aligned}
$$
i.e.
$$
\frac{a}{b^{2}+1}+\frac{b}{c^{2}+1}+\frac{c}{a^{2}+1} \geq \frac{(a \sqrt{a}+b \sqrt{b}+c \sqrt{c})^{2}}{a^{2}\left(b^{2}+1\right)+b^{2}\left(c^{2}+1\right)+c^{2}\left(a^{2}+1\right)}
$$

So it suffices to show that
$$
a^{2}\left(b^{2}+1\right)+b^{2}\left(c^{2}+1\right)+c^{2}\left(a^{2}+1\right) \leq \frac{4}{3}
$$

From the obvious inequality $\left(a^{2}-b^{2}\right)^{2}+\left(b^{2}-c^{2}\right)^{2}+\left(c^{2}-a^{2}\right)^{2} \geq 0$ we deduce that
$$
\begin{equation*}
a^{2} b^{2}+b^{2} c^{2}+c^{2} a^{2} \leq a^{4}+b^{4}+c^{4} \tag{2}
\end{equation*}
$$

Now we have
$$
\begin{aligned}
a^{2} & \left(b^{2}+1\right)+b^{2}\left(c^{2}+1\right)+c^{2}\left(a^{2}+1\right) \\
& =a^{2}+b^{2}+c^{2}+a^{2} b^{2}+b^{2} c^{2}+c^{2} a^{2} \\
& =a^{2}+b^{2}+c^{2}+\frac{3\left(a^{2} b^{2}+b^{2} c^{2}+c^{2} a^{2}\right)}{3} \\
& \stackrel{(2)}{\leq} a^{2}+b^{2}+c^{2}+\frac{2\left(a^{2} b^{2}+b^{2} c^{2}+c^{2} a^{2}\right)+a^{4}+b^{4}+c^{4}}{3} \\
& =a^{2}+b^{2}+c^{2}+\frac{\left(a^{2}+b^{2}+c^{2}\right)^{2}}{3}=1+\frac{1}{3}=\frac{4}{3}
\end{aligned}
$$
as required.
Equality occurs iff $a=b=c=1 / \sqrt{3}$. Therefore, the answer is (B) $\geq$.

