theorems.Theorem:
For any real numbers $a_{1}, a_{2}, \ldots, a_{n}$ and $b_{1}, b_{2}, \ldots, b_{n}$

$$
\begin{gathered}
\left(a_{1}^{2}+a_{2}^{2}+\ldots+a_{n}^{2}\right)\left(b_{1}^{2}+b_{2}^{2}+\ldots+b_{n}^{2}\right) \geq \\
\geq\left(a_{1} b_{1}+a_{2} b_{2}+\ldots+a_{n} b_{n}\right)^{2}
\end{gathered}
$$

with equality if and only if $a_{i}$ and $b_{i}$ are proportional, $i=1,2, \ldots, n$.

solution:
By the Cauchy-Schwarz inequality we have
$$
\begin{align*}
\frac{a}{b+1}+\frac{b}{c+1}+\frac{c}{a+1} & =\frac{a^{2}}{a(b+1)}+\frac{b^{2}}{b(c+1)}+\frac{c^{2}}{c(a+1)} \\
& \geq \frac{(a+b+c)^{2}}{a(b+1)+b(c+1)+c(a+1)} \\
& =\frac{(a+b+c)^{2}}{a b+b c+c a+a+b+c} \tag{1}
\end{align*}
$$

Also we have
$$
\begin{equation*}
a b+b c+c a \leq \frac{(a+b+c)^{2}}{3} \tag{2}
\end{equation*}
$$

By (1) and (2) we get
$$
\frac{a}{b+1}+\frac{b}{c+1}+\frac{c}{a+1} \geq \frac{(a+b+c)^{2}}{\frac{(a+b+c)^{2}}{3}+a+b+c}=\frac{3(a+b+c)}{3+a+b+c}
$$

Equality occurs iff $a=b=c$. Therefore, the answer is (B) $\geq$.

By Hölder's inequality for $n=2$ and $p=q=2$, we obtain
$$
