Solution. Consider the random variables $\widetilde{\xi}:=\frac{\xi-\mathrm{E} \xi}{\sqrt{D \xi}}$ and $\widetilde{\eta}:=\frac{\eta-E \eta}{\sqrt{D \eta}}$. Then $\mathbf{E} \widetilde{\xi}=\mathbf{E} \widetilde{\eta}=0, \mathbf{D} \widetilde{\xi}=\mathbf{D} \widetilde{\eta}=1$ and

$$
\begin{aligned}
& \mathrm{P}\{|\xi-\mathrm{E} \xi| \geqslant \varepsilon \sqrt{\mathrm{D} \xi} \text { or }|\eta-\mathrm{E} \eta| \geqslant \varepsilon \sqrt{\mathrm{D} \eta}\}= \\
& \quad=\mathrm{P}\{|\widetilde{\xi}| \geqslant \varepsilon \text { or }|\widetilde{\eta}| \geqslant \varepsilon\}=\mathrm{P}\left\{\max \left\{\widetilde{\xi}^{2}, \widetilde{\eta}^{2}\right\} \geqslant \varepsilon^{2}\right\}
\end{aligned}
$$

By Chebyshev's inequality, the last probability is no greater than $\frac{\mathrm{E} \max \left\{\tilde{\xi}^{2}, \widetilde{\eta}^{2}\right\}}{\varepsilon^{2}}$, which, by problem I.4.8, is no greater than $\frac{\left(1+\sqrt{1-\rho^{2}}\right)}{\varepsilon^{2}}$, since the correlation coefficient of the variables $\xi$ and $\eta$ is the same as that of the variables $\widetilde{\xi}$ and $\widetilde{\eta}$.

Equality in the bound $C = 1 + \sqrt{1-\rho^2}$ is achieved when $\rho = 0$, i.e., when $\xi$ and $\eta$ are uncorrelated. In this case, $C = 2$ is the minimal constant such that the inequality always holds, and this gives the maximum possible value of $C$ over all possible $\rho$.

Therefore, the answer is $C = 2.$.

