We first used the inequality $a b \leqslant \frac{a^{p}}{p}+\frac{b^{q}}{q}$ for $p=\frac{W_{n}}{W_{n-1}}$ and $q=\frac{W_{n}}{w_{n}}$ (it is clear that $1 / p+1 / q=1$), and then the induction hypothesis.

Second solution. We will use the inequality $1 + x < e^{x}$ for $x \neq 0$ (problem 28.49). Define the numbers $b_{1}, \ldots, b_{n}$ such that the equalities $a_{i}=\left(1+b_{i}\right) \frac{w_{1} a_{1}+\ldots+w_{n} a_{n}}{W}$ hold. Then

$$
w_{i} b_{i}=w_{i} \frac{a_{i}\left(w_{1}+\ldots+w_{n}\right)-w_{1} a_{1}-\ldots-w_{n} a_{n}}{w_{1} a_{1}+\ldots+w_{n} a_{n}}
$$

therefore $w_{1} b_{1}+\ldots+w_{n} b_{n}=0$. It is easy to see that if not all the numbers $a_{1}, \ldots, a_{n}$ are equal, then at least one of the numbers $b_{1}, \ldots, b_{n}$ is different from zero. In this case,

$$
\begin{aligned}
\left(a_{1}^{w_{1}} \ldots a_{n}^{w_{n}}\right)^{1 / W}= & \frac{w_{1} a_{1}+\ldots+w_{n} a_{n}}{W}\left(\left(1+b_{1}\right)^{w_{1}} \ldots\left(1+b_{n}\right)^{w_{n}}\right)^{1 / W}< \\
