\end{aligned}
$$

At the same time, we have proved that the required inequality is strict if not all the numbers $a_{1}, \ldots, a_{n}$ are equal.

Equality holds if and only if $a_1 = a_2 = \ldots = a_n$, in which case both sides of the inequality are equal and $C = 1$ is achieved. This gives the minimum value of $C$ for which the inequality always holds.

