problem:
Determine the minimal real constant $C$ such that for any function $f(x)$ defined for $x \geq 0$ and satisfying the following conditions:
(1) For any $x, y \geq 0$, $f(x) f(y) \leq y^2 f\left(\frac{x}{2}\right) + x^2 f\left(\frac{y}{2}\right)$;
(2) There exists a constant $M > 0$ such that $|f(x)| \leq M$ for $0 \leq x \leq 1$;
it holds that $f(x) \leq C x^2$ for all $x \geq 0$.

solution:
The answer is affirmative. Take the function $f(x)=x^{3}+x k(x)$. Here $k(x)$ is a bounded, non-constant function with a period of 2 defined on $\mathbf{R}$ (for example, $k(x)=\sin (\pi x), k(x)=x-[x], \cdots$). For such an $f(x)$ and any real number $x$, we have
$$
\begin{aligned}
& (x-1) f(x+1)-(x+1) f(x-1) \\
= & (x-1)(x+1)^{3}-(x+1)(x-1)^{3}+\left(x^{2}-1\right)[k(x+1)-k(x-1)] \\
= & 4 x\left(x^{2}-1\right)+0 . \quad \text { (since } k(x) \text { has a period of } 2 \text { ) }
\end{aligned}
$$

Equality holds for all $x$ when $f(x) = x^3 + x k(x)$ with $k(x)$ any bounded, period-2 function, and the expression achieves exactly $4x(x^2-1)$. Thus, the maximum value of $C$ for which the inequality always holds is $C = 4$.

Therefore, the answer is $C = 4$.

